The voice read, continued at the same depth. Both chapters pass the mechanical rules. By file and line:

00b-how-to-read-a-map.md
- 5, 7: third person ("which the editor or any reader can contest", "The editor has used maps for years ... in which he describes"). In his voice: "I have used maps for years: ...". This paragraph is the most natural first-person passage in the chapter; it is his history.
- 7, 89: the sources are "the researcher's dossier"; the writer's rules want public sources named and dated. The talk at the Open Security Summit has no year, and "Why I love Wardley maps" (October 2026) and his site on maps should be named with their addresses, not through the dossier.
- 89: "The editor's own site on maps warns about the book's maps in particular": in his voice, "My own site on maps warns ...", which is also stronger: he is warning about his own book.
- 17, 19, 23, 41, 95, 97: the maps label the user "the editor", and the prose follows. In first person the prose would say "I" while the map says "the editor"; either the map labels change to "me" (the designer's and the conductor's pipeline) or the prose keeps "the editor" when it reads a map aloud. A choice for the revision, not a fault.
- 29: "everything we use": the one "we" that should stay "we" (everyone, not the team).

01-pause-all-actions.md
- 7: "the editor's own hand is everywhere in the record ... at his word": "my hand", "at my word".
- 9: "The editor, in the conductor's session, had said": the pivot of the chapter; in his voice, "I told the conductor: ..." with his own sentence quoted as it stands.
- 29: "It is the conductor's report of what the editor told it ... a human telling": here first person does more than change a pronoun. The record cannot say where the 95% came from; only he can. "Honest about where he stands" means he says, in one sentence, what he was looking at when he said 95%, or that he no longer knows. That is his to write, not ours to guess.

